title: "Replace secp256k1 with k256 in sp-core, add v3 host functions with low-S enforcement (BIP-62 / EIP-2)"
doc:
  - audience: Runtime Dev
    description: |
      Unifies `sp-core`'s ECDSA implementation to use `k256` exclusively, removing the
      `#[cfg(feature = "std")]` / `#[cfg(not(feature = "std"))]` bifurcation that previously
      switched between `secp256k1` (std) and `k256` (no_std). The `secp256k1` crate is removed
      from `sp-core` dependencies entirely. `k256/precomputed-tables` is enabled under the `std`
      feature for host-side performance.

      **New v3 host functions** — Four new host function versions are added in `sp-io` that use
      `k256` and enforce low-S signatures (BIP-62 / EIP-2):
      - `ecdsa_verify` v3 — rejects high-S signatures before delegating to `Pair::verify()`
      - `ecdsa_verify_prehashed` v2 — rejects high-S signatures before delegating to
        `Pair::verify_prehashed()`
      - `secp256k1_ecdsa_recover` v3 — rejects high-S, uses k256 for recovery (uncompressed)
      - `secp256k1_ecdsa_recover_compressed` v3 — rejects high-S, uses k256 for recovery
        (compressed)

      Existing v1 (libsecp256k1) and v2 (secp256k1) host functions are completely unchanged for
      backward compatibility. The `secp256k1` crate remains a dependency of `sp-io` for these
      v2 recovery functions (`secp256k1_ecdsa_recover`, `secp256k1_ecdsa_recover_compressed`).

      **Note:** `ecdsa_verify` v2 calls `Pair::verify()`, which now implicitly uses `k256`
      after the sp-core unification. This is accepted because the mathematical result is
      identical — both libraries implement the same secp256k1 curve operations.

      **Host function library layering:**
      | Version | Library | Low-S |
      |---------|---------|-------|
      | v1 | `libsecp256k1` | No |
      | v2 | `secp256k1` (rust-bitcoin) | No |
      | v3 | `k256` | **Yes** |

      **Claims pallet** — Migrated from `secp256k1` to `k256`. The test/benchmark helpers
      (`secp_utils`, `mock.rs`, `benchmarking.rs`) now use `k256::ecdsa::SigningKey` instead of
      `secp256k1::SecretKey`. The `secp256k1` crate is removed from `polkadot-runtime-common`.

      **sp_core::ecdsa** — A public constant `SECP256K1_HALF_ORDER` and helper function
      `is_signature_normalized()` are available for checking whether a 65-byte ECDSA signature
      has its S value in the lower half of the curve order. `Pair::sign_prehashed()` performs
      explicit low-S normalization and includes a debug assertion. The `Drop` impl for
      `GenericPair` is removed since k256's `SigningKey` implements `ZeroizeOnDrop`.

      **pallet-revive** — `recover_eth_address()` rejects high-S signatures before calling
      `secp256k1_ecdsa_recover`, matching Ethereum post-Homestead validation (EIP-2). The
      `ECRecover` precompile is intentionally unchanged. The unused `secp256k1` dev-dependency
      is removed.

      **sp-consensus-beefy** — `ecdsa_crypto::BeefyAuthorityId::verify()` rejects high-S
      signatures before recovery, ensuring canonical form for Ethereum-side light clients.

      `Pair::verify()` and `Pair::verify_prehashed()` are not changed to preserve backward
      compatibility with historical block verification.

  - audience: Runtime User
    description: |
      New v3 host functions for ECDSA verification and recovery are available that enforce
      low-S signature normalization per BIP-62 / EIP-2. Runtimes can opt into these stricter
      functions to reject malleable signatures.

      EVM transactions submitted to pallet-revive now require low-S signatures per EIP-2. All
      standard Ethereum signing libraries already produce low-S signatures by default, so no
      action is needed for well-behaved signers. Transactions with non-canonical (high-S)
      signatures will be rejected at validation time.

      BEEFY ECDSA signature verification now also requires low-S. Validators using standard
      signing tooling are unaffected.

crates:
  - name: sp-core
    bump: major
  - name: sp-io
    bump: minor
  - name: polkadot-runtime-common
    bump: patch
  - name: pallet-revive
    bump: patch
  - name: sp-consensus-beefy
    bump: patch
